<body class="marketing-page">
	<SiteNav />

	<main>

	<section class="hero alt-hero">
		<div class="hero-badge">Alternatives · Self-Hosted Email</div>
		<h1>The Google Workspace alternative<br/><span>you actually own.</span></h1>
		<p class="alt-lead">
			Google Workspace keeps your mailboxes on Google's cloud and bills you for
			every seat, forever. Vectis Mail is a self-hosted email server you run on
			<em>your</em> own VPS: full IMAP/SMTP mailboxes on your domain, a REST API,
			DKIM/SPF/DMARC by default, and a flat price that never scales with headcount.
			It's email infrastructure — Gmail and your mail server on your terms — so if
			you want to own your email and stop paying per user, this is the switch.
		</p>
		<div class="hero-buttons" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
			<a href="/getting-started/installation/" class="btn-primary">Install Vectis Mail free</a>
			<a href="/pricing/" class="btn-secondary">View pricing</a>
		</div>
		<div class="alt-meta" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
			<span>Last updated <time datetime={dateModified}>7 July 2026</time></span>
			<span>·</span>
			<span>By the Vectis Mail team</span>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 2rem;">
		<h2>Why teams move to Vectis Mail</h2>
		<p class="subtitle">Where per-seat cloud billing and rented mailboxes start to hurt.</p>
		<div class="feature-card">
			<ul style="margin-bottom: 0;">
				<li>Your Workspace bill grows every time you hire, and a flat price that ignores headcount would change the math.</li>
				<li>Data custody or jurisdiction matters — you want mail in your own Postgres and IMAP store under your chosen jurisdiction, not on Google's US-primary cloud under an attestation.</li>
				<li>You want zero lock-in: plain IMAP/SMTP on your own domain means leaving is a DNS change, not a migration project.</li>
				<li>You need a real transactional sending API and inbound webhooks alongside human mailboxes — one platform for both.</li>
				<li>You already run non-Google tools for documents and meetings (or don't need them), so the suite isn't load-bearing.</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
		<p style="font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--sl-color-gray-3); margin-top: 1rem;">
			One honest scope note: Vectis Mail is email infrastructure — the Gmail-and-mail-server
			part of a suite — not a full productivity suite. It does not include Docs, Sheets,
			Slides, Drive, Meet, or Calendar. That's our scope by design; keep or pair those tools
			separately if your team needs them.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>At a glance</h2>
		<p class="subtitle">Side-by-side on the dimensions that actually decide the call. Current as of June 2026.</p>
		<div class="compare-table-wrap">
			<table class="compare-table">
				<thead>
					<tr><th></th><th>Vectis Mail</th><th>Google Workspace</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><th scope="row">What it is</th><td>Self-hosted <strong>email server</strong></td><td>Cloud productivity suite</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Pricing model</th><td><strong>Flat</strong> — $0 self-host / $39 per tenant/mo</td><td>Per user / month (~$7–$22/user)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Scales with headcount?</th><td><strong>No</strong> — same price at 10 or 500 mailboxes</td><td>Yes — linear with every seat</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Where your mail lives</th><td><strong>Your server / your Postgres + IMAP store</strong></td><td>Google's cloud (US-primary)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Mail protocols</th><td><strong>Standard IMAP / POP3 / SMTP</strong></td><td>IMAP/SMTP supported; ecosystem-optimised for Gmail</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Lock-in</th><td><strong>None</strong> — plain IMAP on your domain</td><td>Suite + admin + identity ecosystem</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Email on your domain</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Docs / Sheets / Slides</th><td>No (email only)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Meet / video</th><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Drive / Calendar</th><td>No (CalDAV on Phase-4 roadmap)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">REST API + inbound webhooks</th><td><strong>Yes</strong> — domain-scoped keys, HMAC-signed</td><td>Gmail API exists; no flat-priced send API</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Deliverability ownership</th><td><strong>You</strong> — your IP, your reputation to warm</td><td>Google — pre-warmed, managed</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Spam filtering</th><td><strong>Rspamd + optional ClamAV</strong> — yours to tune</td><td>Google-managed, no tuning access</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Ops burden</th><td>You run a VPS</td><td>Zero — fully managed</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">SSO</th><td>OIDC (Pro) · SAML 2.0 (Enterprise)</td><td>Built-in Google identity + SAML</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--sl-color-gray-3); margin-top: 0.5rem;">
			Google Workspace plan prices per Google Workspace's published pricing, retrieved June 2026; annual-commit per-user/month. Vectis Mail Starter ($0) covers up to 3 domains and 25 mailboxes/domain; Pro is $39 USD/tenant/month plus a $5–$20/mo VPS.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Two honest trade-offs</h2>
		<div class="feature-card">
			<p style="margin-bottom: 0.75rem;"><strong>Ownership, not third-party scanning.</strong> With Vectis Mail your mail sits in your own Postgres and IMAP store on a server you run, so no third party holds it, scans it, or can be compelled to hand it over; mail in transit is protected by TLS. The point isn't a claim about anyone's policy — ownership removes the question entirely, because the data never leaves your infrastructure.</p>
			<p><strong>Deliverability is yours to earn.</strong> Day one on a fresh VPS IP, you need 2–4 weeks of <a href="/deliverability/" style="color: var(--sl-color-accent);">IP warmup</a> before sending volume to Gmail/Outlook inboxes. You don't take a day-one hit migrating: the dual-send playbook keeps your current provider delivering while your new IP warms. After warmup, inbox placement comes down to your IP reputation and DNS hygiene — DKIM/SPF/DMARC/MTA-STS, which Vectis Mail configures correctly by default — not the platform you send from.</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>How they differ</h2>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Own your email instead of renting it per seat</h3>
		<p>
			Google Workspace prices per user per month. As of early 2026, Business Starter
			is about $7/user/month on an annual plan ($8.40 billed monthly), Business
			Standard about $14/user/month ($16.80 monthly), and Business Plus about
			$22/user/month ($26.40 monthly). Google raised prices roughly 17–22% in 2025
			when it bundled Gemini AI into the paid plans. The bill is linear: every new
			hire is another line item, forever.
		</p>
		<p>
			Vectis Mail prices per tenant — your organisation — not per seat. Starter is
			free forever (up to 3 domains, 25 mailboxes per domain). Pro is $39 USD per
			tenant per month for unlimited domains and unlimited mailboxes, plus a
			$5–$20/month VPS to host it. The total is flat whether you run 10 mailboxes or
			500. One Pro subscription covers every Vectis Mail install your organisation
			operates.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">The TCO math at 10 and 50 users</h3>
		<div class="compare-table-wrap">
			<table class="compare-table">
				<thead>
					<tr><th>Mailboxes</th><th>Google Workspace (Business Standard, annual)</th><th>Vectis Mail (Pro + $20/mo VPS)</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><th scope="row">10 users</th><td>~$140/mo · ~$1,680/yr</td><td><strong>$59/mo flat · $708/yr</strong></td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">50 users</th><td>~$700/mo · ~$8,400/yr</td><td><strong>$59/mo flat · $708/yr</strong></td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">200 users</th><td>~$2,800/mo · ~$33,600/yr</td><td><strong>$59/mo flat · $708/yr</strong></td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--sl-color-gray-3); margin-top: 0.5rem;">
			Workspace figures use Business Standard at ~$14/user/month (annual commit), retrieved June 2026. The honest counter: that Workspace spend also buys Docs, Sheets, Meet, Drive, and zero operational burden. Vectis Mail is email only — so the fair comparison is "email versus email," with the suite priced separately if you still need it.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Planning the move around scope</h3>
		<p>
			Vectis Mail owns the email lane; documents and video live elsewhere. That makes
			the switch a planning decision, and it usually takes one of three shapes:
			(1) <strong>email-only move</strong> — your team already uses other tools for
			docs/calls, so you move mailboxes to Vectis Mail and go;
			(2) <strong>keep the suite, move the mail</strong> — you keep another tool for
			docs/video but reclaim email custody and flat pricing;
			(3) <strong>pair with open tools</strong> — Vectis Mail for mail, plus something
			like Nextcloud/OnlyOffice for documents and Jitsi for video. Calendar/contacts
			(CalDAV/CardDAV) is on the Vectis Mail Phase-4 roadmap, not shipping today.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Data custody and jurisdiction</h3>
		<p>
			With Workspace, your mail lives on Google's infrastructure (US-primary) under
			Google's terms — a policy you can read but not control. Vectis Mail replaces
			that with ownership: because you run the server, no third party holds your mail,
			scans it, or can be compelled to hand it over. The custody question simply
			doesn't arise.
		</p>
		<p>
			With Vectis Mail, the data is wherever you deploy the server. Your Postgres
			holds the metadata and audit trail; your filesystem holds the IMAP message
			store. GDPR, data-residency, and sovereignty conversations become "which region
			did you deploy in" instead of "do you accept the vendor's attestation." For
			regulated industries and EU public-sector buyers, that shift is frequently the
			whole reason to self-host.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">No lock-in: plain IMAP/SMTP on your domain</h3>
		<p>
			Workspace is sticky by design — the admin console, identity, Drive sharing, and
			the suite all reinforce staying. Vectis Mail is the opposite: mailboxes are
			vanilla Dovecot over standard IMAP/POP3, sending is standard SMTP, and the
			address is on <em>your</em> domain. Migrating in or out is an <code>imapsync</code>
			run and a DNS change, not a project. You are never holding the data hostage to
			keep a customer, and neither are we.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Admin, API, and full control</h3>
		<p>
			Workspace gives you a polished admin console for a managed service. Vectis Mail
			gives you the keys to the stack: a React admin UI, a 40+ endpoint REST API
			(sending, mailboxes, domains, webhooks, analytics) with domain-scoped keys, a
			single declarative <code>config.yaml</code> as the source of truth, and
			atomic updates with automatic rollback. You can tune Postfix, write custom
			Rspamd rules, and automate everything via the API or Terraform — control that a
			managed suite, by definition, doesn't hand you.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Deliverability — the real trade-off</h3>
		<p>
			This is the real trade-off, and we'll say it plainly. A managed provider sends
			from warm, pooled IPs, so there's no day-one reputation curve. Self-hosting on a
			fresh VPS IP means your IP has no reputation on day one; Gmail, Outlook, and Yahoo
			will throttle or spam-fold most mail until you've established a pattern over
			2–4 weeks. Vectis Mail ships DKIM,
			SPF, DMARC, and MTA-STS configured correctly by default, and the
			<a href="/deliverability/">dual-send migration playbook</a> means you keep
			Workspace delivering while your new IP warms — so there's no day-one gap, just
			an overlap window you control. After warmup, inbox placement comes down to your
			IP reputation and DNS hygiene — DKIM/SPF/DMARC/MTA-STS, correct rDNS, and a clean
			sending pattern — rather than the platform you send from.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Migrating from Google Workspace to Vectis Mail</h2>
		<p>
			The mailbox move is mechanical; the constraint is the IP warmup calendar (and,
			if you use the suite, deciding what replaces Docs/Meet). The typical sequence:
		</p>
		<ol class="alt-steps">
			<li><strong>Decide the suite question first.</strong> Confirm what replaces Docs, Sheets, Meet, and Drive — keep them, switch to open tools, or accept you don't need them. Vectis Mail handles only email.</li>
			<li><strong>Stand up Vectis Mail</strong> on a fresh VPS via the <a href="/getting-started/installation/">installation guide</a>. Add your domain(s) and publish the DKIM/SPF/DMARC records Vectis Mail generates.</li>
			<li><strong>Sync mailbox data</strong> with <code>imapsync</code> from Gmail/Workspace IMAP into Vectis Mail's Dovecot — messages, folders, and flags transfer over standard IMAP.</li>
			<li><strong>Run in parallel during warmup.</strong> Keep Workspace receiving while your new IP warms; cut sending over gradually following the IP warmup schedule.</li>
			<li><strong>Cut MX over</strong> to the Vectis Mail server once warm. Mail starts flowing through Vectis Mail as DNS propagates.</li>
			<li><strong>Verify + decommission.</strong> Watch logs and deliverability for a week, then close down Workspace mail (and keep or cancel the suite per step 1).</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			Realistic timeline: a few hours of setup, then a 2–4 week warmup window that
			runs in the background while Workspace keeps serving. See the
			<a href="/guides/deliverability/">deliverability guide</a> for the calendar.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
		<div class="alt-faq">
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Is there a self-hosted alternative to Google Workspace?</h3>
				<p>
					For the email half, yes — Vectis Mail. It's a self-hosted email server
					(Postfix, Dovecot, Rspamd) you run on your own VPS, with full IMAP/SMTP
					mailboxes on your domain, a REST API, inbound webhooks, and
					DKIM/SPF/DMARC configured by default. Pricing is flat: <a href="/pricing/">$0
					self-hosted Starter, or $39/month per tenant for Pro</a> — never per user,
					never per email. The honest caveat: it replaces Gmail and your mail server,
					not the whole suite. No Docs, Sheets, Slides, Drive, Meet, or Calendar. If
					your team lives in those, keep them or pair Vectis Mail alongside.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Can Vectis Mail replace Gmail?</h3>
				<p>
					Yes. Vectis Mail hosts full IMAP/POP3/SMTP mailboxes on your own domain with
					Roundcube webmail included, so users keep <code>name@yourdomain.com</code>
					and connect from Outlook, Apple Mail, Thunderbird, or any mobile client over
					standard protocols. It's the Gmail half of Workspace on your server. What you
