					ecosystem lock-in.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does Vectis Mail replace Google Docs, Sheets, and Meet?</h3>
				<p>
					No — and this is the point. Vectis Mail is <strong>email only</strong>. No
					Docs, Sheets, Slides, Drive, Meet, or Calendar, and it doesn't try to be
					them. Google Workspace is a full productivity suite; Vectis Mail replaces the
					email server inside it. If you depend on real-time collaborative documents or
					Google Meet, either keep Workspace for everyone, keep the suite and move only
					mailboxes to Vectis Mail, or pair Vectis Mail with separate document/video
					tools (Nextcloud, OnlyOffice, Jitsi). Choose Vectis Mail when email
					sovereignty and flat pricing outweigh a single bundled suite.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>How much does Google Workspace cost versus self-hosting email?</h3>
				<p>
					Workspace bills per user per month. As of early 2026: Business Starter ~$7/user/mo
					annual ($8.40 monthly), Business Standard ~$14/user/mo ($16.80 monthly),
					Business Plus ~$22/user/mo ($26.40 monthly) — linear with headcount. Vectis
					Mail is flat: $0 Starter or $39/month per tenant for Pro, plus a $5–$20/month
					VPS. At 50 users, Business Standard is roughly $700/month ($8,400/year); the
					equivalent Vectis Mail Pro install is about $59/month all-in. The fair caveat:
					that Workspace price also buys Docs, Meet, Drive, and zero ops — a self-hosted
					email server doesn't.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Who can read my mail if I self-host with Vectis Mail?</h3>
				<p>
					You do, and no third party. The Vectis Mail model is custody: because you run
					the server, your messages sit in your own Postgres and IMAP store, wherever you
					deploy — no external provider holds your mail, scans it, or can be compelled to
					hand it over. Mail in transit is protected by TLS. With a hosted suite your mail
					lives on the vendor's infrastructure under a policy you can inspect but not
					control; with Vectis Mail the boundary is your own server, so the question
					becomes "where did you deploy it," not "do you trust the vendor's attestation."
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

